# aicomply is a DLP / content-normalization library. Its normalization tests and
# benchmark corpus + generator MUST contain invisible-unicode and homoglyph
# characters as fixtures -- those characters are the very inputs the normalize
# layer exists to fold (NFKC, zero-width strip, homoglyph fold). HackMyAgent's
# UNICODE-STEGO-001 / UNICODE-STEGO-005 checks fire on these intentional
# samples. This is the same situation HMA documents in its own .hmaignore
# ("scanner contains the detection patterns that match the threats it detects").
#
# Suppression is path-scoped, not a global check-ID disable, so UNICODE-STEGO
# still fires on any NEW source file outside these intentional-fixture paths.

# Normalization unit-test fixtures: zero-width, full-width, and homoglyph samples by design
src/normalize/__tests__/
src/classifier/dual-layer/__tests__/dual-layer-normalize.test.ts
python/tests/test_normalize.py

# Adversarial accuracy corpus + its generator: intentionally evasion-shaped inputs
bench/
# Conformance cases are seeded from that corpus, so they carry the same samples
pack/conformance/

# The pattern pack holds the detection regexes as JSON strings (for example the
# AWS access-key-id shape `\b(AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16})\b`). AST-CRED-003 reads such a
# regex source as a hardcoded secret. These are pattern definitions, not
# secrets; the Python file is a byte-identical copy checked by `npm run
# pack:check`.
pack/patterns.json
python/src/aicomply/pack/patterns.json

# The npm package ships README.md, SECURITY.md, STATUS.md and docs/ but not this
# file, so a scan of the installed package applies none of these rules. Those
# Markdown files name a zero-width or Cyrillic look-alike character by its code
# point (for example U+200B) instead of embedding it, and no rule here names
# them.

# AIM-001 reads the `ai-agent` keyword in package.json as a sign that this
# project is an agent, and asks for an agent identity file (agent-card.json,
# agent.json or aim.json). aicomply is a library and CLI that classifies the
# input and output of other agents; it does not run as an agent, so it has no
# identity of its own to declare. The rule names that one check on that one
# file, so every other check still reads package.json. Remove it if aicomply
# ever ships an agent identity file.
package.json:AIM-001  # library and CLI, not an agent: no agent identity to declare
